Shark Attacks Data Analysis

 Project Overview

This project analyzes shark attack data to understand patterns and trends in shark attack incidents across different locations, time periods, activities, and shark species. The analysis aims to identify factors associated with shark attacks and provide insights into where, when, and under what circumstances these incidents occur.

Business Problem

The objective of this analysis is to understand the patterns and characteristics of reported shark attacks. By analyzing factors such as location, time, activity, victim information, and shark species, the analysis provides insights that can support better awareness, risk assessment, and safety measures in areas where shark attacks occur.

Objectives

- Analyze the overall number of reported shark attacks
- Identify countries and locations with the highest number of reported attacks
- Analyze shark attacks by year and time period
- Identify the activities most commonly associated with shark attacks
- Analyze the distribution of shark attacks by shark species
- Compare fatal and non-fatal incidents
- Identify patterns in shark attack incidents
- Highlight areas where safety awareness may be important

Tools Used

- Microsoft Excel

Dataset

The dataset contains information about reported shark attack incidents, including the date, year, country, location, activity, victim information, injury details, fatality status, and shark species involved where available.

Data Cleaning

The following data preparation steps were carried out:

- Handled missing and incomplete values
- Standardized country and location names
- Corrected data types
- Cleaned and standardized activity categories
- Standardized fatality information
- Cleaned shark species information
- Extracted relevant date and year information
- Checked for inconsistent values
- Prepared the dataset for analysis and visualization

Analysis

The analysis focused on:

- Total number of reported shark attacks
- Shark attacks by country
- Shark attacks by location
- Shark attacks by year
- Shark attacks by activity
- Fatal versus non-fatal shark attacks
- Shark attacks by shark species
- Geographic distribution of shark attacks
- Trends in reported shark attack incidents over time

Dashboard

The final dashboard was created to provide an interactive view of reported shark attack incidents. It allows users to explore patterns based on factors such as location, year, activity, fatality status, and shark species.

Key Findings

- Recorded shark attacks have both increased and reduced substantially over time
- Legs and Feet Are the Most Frequently Injured
- Afternoon Has the Highest Recorded Attack Frequency
- Great White Is the Most Frequently Identified Species
- Missing information is substantial, particularly for shark species, body-part injuries, time and age. This reduces the reliability of some detailed comparisons.

Recommendations

Based on the analysis:

- Increase public awareness and safety education in areas with higher numbers of reported shark attacks.
- Encourage swimmers, surfers, and other water users to follow local safety guidelines and pay attention to warnings about shark activity.
- Improve the consistency and completeness of shark attack reporting to support more reliable analysis.
- Use historical attack patterns to help identify locations and activities where additional safety measures may be appropriate.
- Continue monitoring trends in reported shark attacks to support better risk awareness and decision-making.

Conclusion

The analysis provides insights into the distribution and characteristics of reported shark attacks across different locations, time periods, activities, and shark species. The findings highlight the importance of understanding historical patterns and improving awareness in areas where shark encounters are more frequently reported. While the dataset provides useful information, missing and inconsistent records should be considered when interpreting the results.
